  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on cross-sectional spatial modeling and predictive mapping, which may not fully capture long-term causal dynamics as infrastructure and economic conditions evolve.",
      "what": "This study analyzed infrastructure supply intensity and spatial inequality across 342 Chinese cities using spatial error modeling and geographical convergent cross mapping. It mapped regional patterns, finding that supply intensity increases from west to east, while high spatial inequality is concentrated in northern and southwestern China. The analysis reveals that higher infrastructure supply intensity is associated with lower economic inequality, whereas higher internal infrastructure inequality correlates with greater economic disparity, especially in the eastern region.",
      "why": "It establishes a dual-directional predictive relationship between infrastructure distribution and economic outcomes, demonstrating that infrastructure inequality itself is a key correlate of regional economic disparity rather than just a byproduct of underdevelopment."
    }
